  "descriptions": [
    {
      "source": "DeFiHackLabs",
      "text": "Arbitrary Call / Wrong Approval. Lost: 1 ETH"
    },
    {
      "source": "SlowMist",
      "text": "A user mistakenly approved the SquidMulticall contract (instead of the intended Squid Router contract) with unlimited token allowances. An attacker then called the permissionless run() function on SquidMulticall with crafted calldata to execute transferFrom() from the victim’s approved tokens across multiple chains (ETH, BSC, Arbitrum, Avalanche, etc.). This drained approximately $517K."
    },
    {
      "source": "RevertIQ",
      "text": "On April 7, 2026, an attacker exploited a SquidMulticall contract deployed on Arbitrum One, BNB Chain, Avalanche, Optimism, and Base, draining approximately $517,000. The exploit was enabled by users mistakenly granting token allowances to a permissionless arbitrary-call executor with insufficient access control. The SquidMulticall contract's run() function permitted arbitrary external calls from any sender, lacking restrictions on caller identity, call target, or token spend source. Once a user approved the contract for unlimited token spending, any third party could drain those tokens by crafting transferFrom calls. A specific victim mistakenly appro…"
    }
  ],
